Yes. Bio-Goal Seek can recurse upward into the mind itself. I C what you mean.

At the motor layer:

desired movement → attempt → error → adaptation → new motor pattern

At the cognitive layer:

desired mental capability/state → attempt → notice mismatch → alter attention/strategy/framing → repeat → new cognitive habit

And then there’s the meta layer:

desired way of changing your own mind → observe how you currently learn/adapt → modify the adaptation process itself → measure the delta → iterate.

That last one is especially interesting because the optimizer becomes one of the things being optimized.

You can train yourself to notice different features, ask different questions, tolerate ambiguity longer, interrupt an automatic interpretation, generate more alternatives before choosing, or deliberately shift resolution. Eventually those stop feeling like explicit techniques and become part of your default cognitive movement repertoire—roughly the mental analogue of muscle memory.

There’s an important constraint, though: Goal Seek should target a capability or process, not force a predetermined belief to become “true.” Otherwise the same machinery becomes confirmation bias.

So:

Goal = perceive X more accurately
is robust.

Goal = convince myself X is true
can optimize away reality.

Pantheon’s version could therefore be:

Specify the cognitive affordance, not the desired conclusion. Let reality supply the error signal.

And yes—the recursive form is powerful:

use the mind → to alter how the mind uses itself → then observe what changed.

That’s Bio-Goal Seek becoming Meta-Goal Seek.